**Definition.** Endianness is the order in which the bytes of a multi-byte value are stored in memory. For the 32-bit value `0x01020304` at address `a`:
- **Little-endian** (x86, most ARM setups): `a: 04, a+1: 03, a+2: 02, a+3: 01` (least significant byte at the lowest address).
- **Big-endian** (network byte order, some older CPUs): `a: 01, a+1: 02, a+2: 03, a+3: 04`.

**Why shifts fail.** `x >> 8` and `x & 0xFF` are defined on the *numeric value*, so they give the same result on both systems. Only looking at the stored bytes reveals the difference.

**Code 1: char pointer (allowed to alias any object).**

```c
#include <stdint.h>
int is_little_endian(void) {
    uint32_t x = 1;
    return *(unsigned char *)&x == 1;   // first byte is 1 only if LSB is stored first
}
```

**Code 2: union (well-defined in C; in C++ prefer memcpy).**

```c
int is_little_endian(void) {
    union { uint32_t i; unsigned char c[4]; } u = { 1 };
    return u.c[0] == 1;
}
```

**Code 3: memcpy (safe in C and C++).**

```c
uint32_t x = 1; unsigned char b;
memcpy(&b, &x, 1);
return b == 1;
```

In C++20 you can simply write `std::endian::native == std::endian::little`.

**How it works.** The value `1` has its only set bit in the least significant byte. If that byte sits at the lowest address (the address of `x`), the machine is little-endian.

**Practical notes.** Network protocols use big-endian, so use `htonl/htons/ntohl/ntohs` when sending integers between machines. Endianness also matters for file formats, binary protocols and reading hardware registers. Bit-fields and struct layout are implementation-defined, so do not rely on them. Avoid casting `int*` to `short*` for this purpose, which can break strict aliasing rules.
